// T4 SOUNDNESS gate: the ADDRESS OF A GLOBAL is not an ARC object, so nothing
// may make it "immortal".
//
// goo_make_immortal reads the 16 bytes at `ptr - 16` and writes the IMMORTAL
// sentinel there. That is correct for a goo_alloc'd object and wrong for
// everything else. `&base` is a plain module-level address with no header, so
// those 16 bytes belong to whatever the linker placed before it.
//
// MEASURED, and it is SILENT: .data is writable, so there is no fault and the
// program exits 0. gdb on this exact shape read 0x7ffff7e6f4a0 at `&base - 16`
// before the call and 0xffffffffffffffff after it -- a live libc pointer
// overwritten with the sentinel.
//
// THE GATE IS AN IR CHECK, NOT A RUN. Whether the corruption damages anything
// depends on what the linker happened to place before `base`, so a runtime
// assertion here would pass or fail by layout luck. The emission either happens
// or it does not, so grep the IR for it and the answer is deterministic.
//
// BOTH PATHS, because they were separate copies of the same logic and the fix
// for one did not reach the other: `p = &base` is the STORE path
// (expression/statement/nullable codegen) and `q = &base` on a package-level
// var is the INIT path (codegen_generate_global_init_function). Both now call
// codegen_arc_make_global_immortal.
package main

type T struct{ x int }

var guard int = 424242
var base T

// INIT path: initialized from the address of a global.
var q *T = &base

// STORE path: assigned the address of a global after init.
var p *T

func setp() { p = &base }

func main() {
	base.x = 7
	setp()
	println(p.x)
	println(q.x)
	println(guard)
}
